From: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix: convert the message type from little endian before matching

awg_determine_type_and_padding() compares the raw on-wire header field
against the host-order H1-H4 ranges. The field is __le32 — encrypt_packet()
and the handshake senders all write it through cpu_to_le32() — but nothing
converts it back on the way in.

On little endian the two happen to coincide, so the bug is invisible. On
big endian the receiver reads the four bytes 01 00 00 00 as 0x01000000 and
u32_range_contains() misses every range, including the defaults set in
wg_newlink():

  u32_range_init(&wg->init_header, MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_INITIATION, ...)   /* {1,1} */
  u32_range_init(&wg->resp_header, MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_RESPONSE, ...)     /* {2,2} */
  u32_range_init(&wg->cookie_header, MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_COOKIE, ...)     /* {3,3} */
  u32_range_init(&wg->transport_header, MESSAGE_DATA, ...)              /* {4,4} */

All four message types fall through to the "unknown type" path, so a big
endian router drops every packet the peer sends and no handshake can ever
complete. This is not limited to a custom H1-H4 configuration: the stock
one is equally unmatched.

Mainline WireGuard compares in the little endian domain instead, converting
the constant rather than the value:

  if (SKB_TYPE_LE32(skb) == cpu_to_le32(MESSAGE_DATA))

AmneziaWG replaced that path with ranged headers and lost the conversion;
SKB_TYPE_LE32 is still defined in messages.h and no longer used anywhere.

Affects the en7512 and en7516 targets (KN-2010, KN-2110, KN-2112, KN-2410,
KN-2510, KN-3610); on the little endian and aarch64 targets le32_to_cpu()
is the identity and the generated code is unchanged.

Fixed upstream on feat/awg-3.1 (commit d6d7342f), which has not been merged
to master as of v3.0.20260805. This is that hunk alone, without the AWG 3.1
feature work around it.

Signed-off-by: Keenetic SDK Builder <developers@keenetic.com>
---
--- a/src/receive.c
+++ b/src/receive.c
@@ -56,7 +56,7 @@
 	if (skb->len == padding + sizeof(struct message_handshake_initiation) &&
 		(ptr = skb_header_pointer(skb, padding, sizeof(buf), buf)) != NULL &&
 		u32_range_contains(wg->init_header,
-			awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash))) {
+			le32_to_cpu(awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash)))) {
 		*res_padding = padding;
 		*res_type = MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_INITIATION;
 		return sizeof(struct message_handshake_initiation);
@@ -66,7 +66,7 @@
 	if (skb->len == padding + sizeof(struct message_handshake_response) &&
 		(ptr = skb_header_pointer(skb, padding, sizeof(buf), buf)) != NULL &&
 		u32_range_contains(wg->resp_header,
-			awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash))) {
+			le32_to_cpu(awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash)))) {
 		*res_padding = padding;
 		*res_type = MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_RESPONSE;
 		return sizeof(struct message_handshake_response);
@@ -76,7 +76,7 @@
 	if (skb->len == padding + sizeof(struct message_handshake_cookie) &&
 		(ptr = skb_header_pointer(skb, padding, sizeof(buf), buf)) != NULL &&
 		u32_range_contains(wg->cookie_header, 
-			awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash))) {
+			le32_to_cpu(awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash)))) {
 		*res_padding = padding;
 		*res_type = MESSAGE_HANDSHAKE_COOKIE;
 		return sizeof(struct message_handshake_cookie);
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@
 	if (skb->len >= padding + MESSAGE_MINIMUM_LENGTH &&
 		(ptr = skb_header_pointer(skb, padding, sizeof(buf), buf)) != NULL &&
 		u32_range_contains(wg->transport_header,
-			awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash))) {
+			le32_to_cpu(awg_decoded_type(ptr, hash)))) {
 		*res_padding = padding;
 		*res_type = MESSAGE_DATA;
 		return sizeof(struct message_data);
